WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
Our garden sensor sent a reading

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.510
over Wi-Fi every ten seconds, so

3
00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.470
we could watch it live.

4
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.730
It was exciting, for four days.

5
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.620
Then two brand-new batteries were flat.

6
00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.290
Today, you'll learn to make

7
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.590
them last for years.

8
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.150
Today: power from batteries and the sun,

9
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.850
and why the radio eats the battery.

10
00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.890
Think of a battery as a tank of

11
00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.370
charge, measured in milliamp-hours.

12
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.520
Two AA batteries hold about two

13
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.640
thousand five hundred.

14
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.790
Every part of the device drinks from that

15
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.430
tank, and its life in hours is the

16
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.670
capacity divided by the average current.

17
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:44.440
Now compare.

18
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.710
Asleep, a sensor uses about a

19
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:48.990
hundredth of a milliamp.

20
00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.270
Measuring takes about five

21
00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.550
milliamps, for a tenth of a second.

22
00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.870
Sending over Wi-Fi takes about one hundred

23
00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.970
and twenty milliamps, for two seconds.

24
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.450
One Wi-Fi send uses as much as

25
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.430
nearly seven hours of sleep!

26
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:06.200
Quick check!

27
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.140
A sensor sends every ten seconds, and

28
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.100
its batteries last four days.

29
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.320
What makes the biggest difference: sending

30
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.660
less often, or sleeping more deeply?

31
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.160
Send less often.

32
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.860
Every ten seconds, the radio

33
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.700
takes almost all the energy.

34
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.050
Send once an hour instead, and

35
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.890
the same two batteries can last

36
00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.070
more than three years.

37
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.540
So a good sensor sleeps

38
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.220
almost all the time.

39
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.140
It wakes, measures, sends, and

40
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.620
goes straight back to sleep.

41
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.480
It can also save ten readings and

42
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.100
send them together, or send only

43
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.140
when something changes.

44
00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.850
Outdoors, add a small solar panel

45
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.230
and a rechargeable battery.

46
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.240
By day, the panel runs the sensor

47
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.520
and charges the battery.

48
00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.990
At night, the battery takes over.

49
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.170
Much of Africa and the Middle East gets

50
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.170
four to six hours of strong sun a day.

51
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.390
Then plan for cloudy days.

52
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.980
Say your sensor uses one

53
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.780
hundred milliamp-hours a day.

54
00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.590
The battery should carry it through five

55
00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.210
cloudy days, so five hundred, plus extra,

56
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.070
because no battery gives all of its label.

57
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.680
And on sunny days, the panel

58
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.960
must refill it too.

59
00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:28.690
Your turn.

60
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.520
A solar-powered rain sensor works

61
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.300
all day, but stops every night.

62
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.260
What's most likely wrong?

63
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.110
The battery: at night, only the

64
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.790
battery keeps the sensor going.

65
00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.350
Remember: the radio is the hungriest part.

66
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.420
Sleep most of the time,

67
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.300
and send less often.

68
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.460
And outdoors: sun by day, a battery by

69
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.420
night, sized for cloudy days.

70
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.740
Try it this week: find the milliamp-hours

71
00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.840
on a power bank or a battery label.

72
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.610
Work out how many hours it could

73
00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:07.010
run a device drawing one hundred

74
00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:08.710
milliamps, then ten.

75
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.570
And never crush, pierce or leave a

76
00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.290
lithium battery in hot sun.
